WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.133 --> 00:00:02.133
-Welcome.
Thanks for coming on.

00:00:02.133 --> 00:00:03.267
Looking sharp, buddy.

00:00:03.267 --> 00:00:04.834
-Thank you very much
for having me, sir.

00:00:04.834 --> 00:00:06.767
-Oh, please, I have a lot
to ask you about.

00:00:06.767 --> 00:00:09.067
I know that you're
from New York,

00:00:09.067 --> 00:00:11.767
but you have a lot of family
in Puerto Rico, right?

00:00:11.767 --> 00:00:13.100
-Yes, that's correct.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:15.067
Thank you. Yes.
-Puerto Rico.

00:00:15.067 --> 00:00:17.601
-I am officially
what is called a Nuyorican.

00:00:17.601 --> 00:00:18.667
-Ah.
-Yes, that's me.

00:00:18.667 --> 00:00:20.534
-A Nuyorican.
-Exactly. A Nuyorican.

00:00:20.534 --> 00:00:23.067
-So you must be familiar
with coquito.

00:00:23.067 --> 00:00:24.334
-Oh, of course.
Coquito, yeah.

00:00:24.334 --> 00:00:25.434
It's our drink
during the holidays.

00:00:25.434 --> 00:00:26.567
-You know coquito?
-Coquito?

00:00:26.567 --> 00:00:28.300
-Coquito is my favorite thing.

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:30.167
I was turned on to it,
probably, I don't know,

00:00:30.167 --> 00:00:32.100
maybe 10 years ago
or something like that.

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:34.501
And it's my -- it's like
a Puerto Rican eggnog.

00:00:34.501 --> 00:00:36.367
-Yeah, like during
the holidays, somewhat.

00:00:36.367 --> 00:00:38.300
You know, we have
our certain meals that we have,

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:40.167
and there's definitely going
to be some coquito being --

00:00:40.167 --> 00:00:42.267
You know, you're gonna
have that beverage to drink.

00:00:42.267 --> 00:00:44.968
It's coconut, rum,
little bit of cinnamon,

00:00:44.968 --> 00:00:47.501
and everyone kind of battles out
as who makes the best coquito.

00:00:47.501 --> 00:00:49.300
-Yeah, it's always --
it's always mom.

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:51.000
-Of course.
-Yeah, mom, someone's mom

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:52.300
always gives me a bottle.
-Grandma, mom.

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:53.634
-Grandma, mom.
-Exactly.

00:00:53.634 --> 00:00:54.934
They hook you up with
a homemade bottle of coquito.

00:00:54.934 --> 00:00:56.434
-You can't buy it in stores.
-No.

00:00:56.434 --> 00:00:59.267
-No. Well, I wanted to welcome
you here to "The Tonight Show."

00:00:59.267 --> 00:01:00.534
-What?
-I made us two glasses

00:01:00.534 --> 00:01:01.934
of coquito.
-What?

00:01:01.934 --> 00:01:03.868
[ Cheers and applause ]
-My God.

00:01:03.868 --> 00:01:05.367
-Come on.
My friend, come on.

00:01:05.367 --> 00:01:06.734
-My God.
Well, okay.

00:01:06.734 --> 00:01:08.234
Since we're going there,

00:01:08.234 --> 00:01:09.901
we have to do
this Puerto Rican style,

00:01:09.901 --> 00:01:12.901
so we have to do
the official toast.

00:01:12.901 --> 00:01:14.033
-Oh.
-It goes like this. Follow me.

00:01:14.033 --> 00:01:15.601
-Arriba.
-Arriba.

00:01:15.601 --> 00:01:17.400
-Abajo.
-Abajo.

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:19.534
-Al centro.
-Al centro.

00:01:19.534 --> 00:01:21.667
-Y pa' dentro.
-Y pa' dentro.

00:01:21.667 --> 00:01:23.400
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:26.667
♪♪

00:01:26.667 --> 00:01:28.400
Whoo!
Who made this?

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:29.400
-It's good, right?
-Who made this?

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:30.868
-Someone's mom.
-Someone's mom.

00:01:30.868 --> 00:01:32.434
[ Laughter ]
-Exactly.

00:01:32.434 --> 00:01:34.434
Now, what do we do?
-Okay. Well --

00:01:34.434 --> 00:01:35.734
He's feeling festive.
-Wow!

00:01:35.734 --> 00:01:37.367
-I'm gonna make him
Puerto Rican

00:01:37.367 --> 00:01:39.200
by the end of this episode.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:40.467
-I love it.
-We're gonna call him "Jaime."

00:01:40.467 --> 00:01:43.334
What about some salsa dancing?
-Oh, no.

00:01:43.334 --> 00:01:45.334
-You know how to dance?
-I'm not a good dancer.

00:01:45.334 --> 00:01:47.100
I'm gonna ask for some help.
Roots, my brothers, would you

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:48.834
mind helping me out with a
little something, something?

00:01:48.834 --> 00:01:51.267
[ Cheers and applause ]
Come on. Come on.

00:01:51.267 --> 00:01:52.667
♪♪

00:01:52.667 --> 00:01:54.767
Hey!
All right, here we go.

00:01:54.767 --> 00:01:57.767
So you're gonna go forward
with your left,

00:01:57.767 --> 00:01:59.467
together,
back with your right.

00:01:59.467 --> 00:02:00.868
Nice.

00:02:00.868 --> 00:02:02.167
Boom.

00:02:02.167 --> 00:02:04.200
Five...four...

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:05.234
Give it up!

00:02:05.234 --> 00:02:08.033
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:08.033 --> 00:02:10.501
♪♪

00:02:10.501 --> 00:02:13.167
-Oh!
You know how to dance, buddy.

00:02:13.167 --> 00:02:17.200
Yeah!
You can dance!

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:19.968
-I'm a Puerto Rican boy
raised in a family of women.

00:02:19.968 --> 00:02:22.334
I had no choice.
-Yeah! You can dance!

00:02:22.334 --> 00:02:23.634
You got the moves.
-They were grabbing me up, man.

00:02:23.634 --> 00:02:24.801
Yeah.
-And then here you are,

00:02:24.801 --> 00:02:27.300
playing a Southern gentleman,
Will Trent.

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:29.968
-Yes. Yeah.
-How do --

00:02:29.968 --> 00:02:32.100
How did you go
and get around doing that?

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:35.000
-I, uh -- well, you know,
one of the first things

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:37.634
I wanted to tackle was, like,
"Okay. This guy's from Atlanta."

00:02:37.634 --> 00:02:40.133
You know, I wanted to be
honest about the character.

00:02:40.133 --> 00:02:43.267
And so I got a dialect coach
to help take away

00:02:43.267 --> 00:02:45.200
some of my New York
Puerto Rican sounds...

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:47.200
-Wow!
-And help me sort of dive into

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:50.701
that molasses, beautiful sound
that's rich in the South.

00:02:50.701 --> 00:02:52.234
And so we actually found,
like, a model.

00:02:52.234 --> 00:02:54.300
We found --

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:58.367
We'll know this artist
from Outkast, Andre 3000.

00:02:58.367 --> 00:03:00.267
We used them
just as an inspiration.

00:03:00.267 --> 00:03:01.868
Not trying to copy him,

00:03:01.868 --> 00:03:05.300
but to find the nuance
and the specificity of his sound

00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:06.968
and try to take some of that
and some of the things

00:03:06.968 --> 00:03:08.133
that we put together and create
the sound of Will Trent.

00:03:08.133 --> 00:03:09.634
So you listened to Outkast
before you --

00:03:09.634 --> 00:03:10.901
-I listened to a bunch of --
Well, I listened to a bunch

00:03:10.901 --> 00:03:12.934
of his interviews.
And in those interviews,

00:03:12.934 --> 00:03:14.701
I could -- you know,
I was working with this coach,

00:03:14.701 --> 00:03:16.033
and we were able to create

00:03:16.033 --> 00:03:18.934
this specific sound
that is Will Trent.

00:03:18.934 --> 00:03:20.267
-That's amazing.
-Yeah.

00:03:20.267 --> 00:03:22.100
Now, every time I watch,
I'm gonna think of Andre 3000.

00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:23.634
-I mean, you might pick up
some of it.

00:03:23.634 --> 00:03:25.434
It was more like -- it's nice
to have a foundation

00:03:25.434 --> 00:03:26.801
that you can base
a dialect on.

00:03:26.801 --> 00:03:27.968
So it's not sort of
out of nowhere.

00:03:27.968 --> 00:03:29.167
It's very specific.

00:03:29.167 --> 00:03:31.567
-Do you have any certain words,
like...

00:03:31.567 --> 00:03:33.234
-Well, yeah, they were, like,
phrases, you know,

00:03:33.234 --> 00:03:36.000
like the way he walked,
the way he talked.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:37.968
She was gone when she --
when she was --

00:03:37.968 --> 00:03:40.267
she took the dog for a walk
in the morning, and she was --"

00:03:40.267 --> 00:03:41.968
You know, so you start
sort of putting together...

00:03:41.968 --> 00:03:43.300
-That's right.

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:45.968
-And there's also flow
and a molasses to it,

00:03:45.968 --> 00:03:47.767
a jazz to it
that's really beautiful.

00:03:47.767 --> 00:03:50.501
So, thank you, Andre 3000,
wherever you at.

00:03:50.501 --> 00:03:54.334
-Yeah, that's fantastic.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:54.334 --> 00:03:55.467
A lot of people --
-Excuse me.

00:03:55.467 --> 00:03:57.534
-Oh, please. Of course.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:57.534 --> 00:04:00.300
A lot of people say
in this business...

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:02.267
Ooh, I love coquito.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:02.267 --> 00:04:04.901
..."Don't work with children,
and don't work with dogs."

00:04:04.901 --> 00:04:06.334
-Yeah.
-They're very difficult --

00:04:06.334 --> 00:04:08.033
-Unpredictable. You don't know
what's gonna happen.

00:04:08.033 --> 00:04:10.400
-I disagree.
I had a great time with my --

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:12.968
-By the way...
-They were very talented.

00:04:12.968 --> 00:04:14.400
-Very talented, very talented.
-What is --

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:16.767
Talk to me about
your co-star here, Betty.

00:04:16.767 --> 00:04:18.701
-First of all,
it looks like our prom photo.

00:04:18.701 --> 00:04:21.000
[ Laughter ]

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:22.367
It's like graduation,
prom photo.

00:04:22.367 --> 00:04:25.067
Betty, she's cute.
She's gonna be offended

00:04:25.067 --> 00:04:26.734
that you had other dogs
here tonight with --

00:04:26.734 --> 00:04:28.300
You know, and I'm on,
and she didn't get

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:29.567
to show off her tricks.
-Betty, you're invited

00:04:29.567 --> 00:04:31.334
for next time Ramon comes on.

00:04:31.334 --> 00:04:33.734
-She's amazing.
She's an incredible dog.

00:04:33.734 --> 00:04:35.901
She's a diva, you know.
-Yeah.

00:04:35.901 --> 00:04:38.133
-Yeah. She rolls with a little
Chihuahua entourage.

00:04:38.133 --> 00:04:39.534
There's no eye contact.

00:04:39.534 --> 00:04:42.067
She has her own trailer, her own
groomer, her own glam squad.

00:04:42.067 --> 00:04:44.434
-Wow.
-She comes to set,

00:04:44.434 --> 00:04:47.100
does her scenes very quickly,
and they just --

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:48.667
They fly her in from Florida.
True story.

00:04:48.667 --> 00:04:50.434
All the rest of the stuff I like
to just make up about her,

00:04:50.434 --> 00:04:52.868
but they actually fly
her in from Florida.

00:04:52.868 --> 00:04:54.300
She does a scene,
and they're like constantly

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:55.734
just giving her treats.

00:04:55.734 --> 00:04:57.100
And then she's whisked back
to Florida.

00:04:57.100 --> 00:04:59.901
And I'm like,
"What did I do wrong in life

00:04:59.901 --> 00:05:01.467
that I didn't figure that job?"

00:05:01.467 --> 00:05:02.901
-Like, what happens when you
when you finish your take?

00:05:02.901 --> 00:05:04.234
-Nothing. They're like, "Cut!
We're moving on."

00:05:04.234 --> 00:05:06.167
-Yeah. Yeah. You're like...
-"Got it. We got it."

00:05:06.167 --> 00:05:07.834
-"I want a treat
and a flight to Florida."

00:05:07.834 --> 00:05:09.534
-Exactly. Yeah, yeah.
-Come on.

00:05:09.534 --> 00:05:12.267
-Seriously.
She works for an hour.

00:05:12.267 --> 00:05:15.234
-Betty, please, if you're
watching -- I know you are --

00:05:15.234 --> 00:05:16.667
please come on the show
next time that Ramon

00:05:16.667 --> 00:05:17.868
comes on the show, please.

00:05:17.868 --> 00:05:19.434
Thank you, Betty.
-Officially invited.

00:05:19.434 --> 00:05:20.567
-You are officially invited.

00:05:20.567 --> 00:05:22.667
Can we set up
what "Will Trent's" about,

00:05:22.667 --> 00:05:24.167
for anyone who hasn't seen it?
-Yeah, sure.

00:05:24.167 --> 00:05:25.968
So, I play Will Trent.

00:05:25.968 --> 00:05:28.968
He's a special agent in the
Georgia Bureau of Investigation,

00:05:28.968 --> 00:05:30.267
and he's a character

00:05:30.267 --> 00:05:33.434
that just had a really
complicated childhood, man.

00:05:33.434 --> 00:05:35.667
And what I loved about him
was his resilience.

00:05:35.667 --> 00:05:38.434
You know, he's someone that grew
up in the foster-care system,

00:05:38.434 --> 00:05:40.601
was an orphan, is dyslexic.

00:05:40.601 --> 00:05:43.934
And despite all of these
sort of hurdles in life,

00:05:43.934 --> 00:05:46.601
he was able to rise up
the ranks of the GBI.

00:05:46.601 --> 00:05:48.801
And he's got a very
unique perspective

00:05:48.801 --> 00:05:50.033
as to how he looks at things,

00:05:50.033 --> 00:05:51.133
partly because of what
he's been through.

00:05:51.133 --> 00:05:53.133
So when he arrives
at a crime scene,

00:05:53.133 --> 00:05:55.367
he sort of puts together
the puzzle in his own mind

00:05:55.367 --> 00:05:56.834
visually in a very cool way.

00:05:56.834 --> 00:05:58.567
And I think that was
some of the fun stuff

00:05:58.567 --> 00:06:00.300
visually we get to play with
on the show, too.

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:01.667
-Good man.
I want to show a clip.

00:06:01.667 --> 00:06:05.067
Here's Ramon Rodriguez
in "Will Trent." Take a look.

00:06:05.067 --> 00:06:08.834
-Is that the last bag of heroin
in Georgia?

00:06:08.834 --> 00:06:11.167
-Okay. How do I fix this
for you, Will?

00:06:11.167 --> 00:06:13.167
-Go back in time.
Okay?

00:06:13.167 --> 00:06:16.667
And don't bring drugs
into my house.

00:06:16.667 --> 00:06:19.167
If it's not for you,
then who's it for, huh?

00:06:19.167 --> 00:06:22.567
-[ Sighs ]
I can't tell you that.

00:06:22.567 --> 00:06:26.300
Look, I had a bad idea,
and I'm over it, but I'm clean.

00:06:26.300 --> 00:06:28.334
I'm telling you --
-Tell me!

00:06:28.334 --> 00:06:32.467
♪♪

00:06:32.467 --> 00:06:34.434
You're lying to me.

00:06:34.434 --> 00:06:36.167
♪♪

00:06:36.167 --> 00:06:37.701
And I hate it.

00:06:37.701 --> 00:06:40.634
♪♪

00:06:40.634 --> 00:06:42.133
Maybe it's a good thing
I'm leaving.

00:06:42.133 --> 00:06:44.367
-Yeah, maybe it is.

00:06:44.367 --> 00:06:46.801
♪♪

00:06:46.801 --> 00:06:47.968
-Yeah.

00:06:47.968 --> 00:06:50.968
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:50.968 --> 00:06:53.901
-Next time you come on, pitorro.

00:06:53.901 --> 00:06:55.767
-Pitorro. You hear this?

00:06:55.767 --> 00:06:57.634
He's saying it live.
He wants pitorro.

00:06:57.634 --> 00:07:00.133
For those who don't know,
that's Puerto Rican moonshine.

00:07:00.133 --> 00:07:02.167
-Yeah.
-My man wants to party.

00:07:02.167 --> 00:07:03.534
-Let's party.
Next time you come back.

00:07:03.534 --> 00:07:07.267
Ramon Rodriguez, everybody.
"Will Trent" airs Tuesdays

00:07:07.267 --> 00:07:09.501
at 10:00 p.m., on ABC.

00:07:09.501 --> 00:07:11.968
More "Tonight Show"
after the break. Stick around.

